[{"id":"31221","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following industry was emphasized in the second five year plan of India?</span></p>","option_1":"IT industry","option_2":"Food processing industry","option_3":"Heavy and basic industry","option_4":"Tourism industry","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Heavy and basic industry.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Second Five-Year Plan (1956-61), based on the Mahalanobis model, laid strong emphasis on rapid industrialization with a focus on the development of heavy and basic industries such as steel, machine-building, and capital goods, to build a self-reliant industrial base.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31222","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Who led the Ahom forces in the Battle of Saraighat?</span></p>","option_1":"Lachit Barphukan","option_2":"Ram Singh","option_3":"Purandar Singh","option_4":"Mir Jumla","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Lachit Barphukan.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Battle of Saraighat (1671) was fought on the Brahmaputra river between the Ahom kingdom and the Mughal Empire. The Ahom forces were led by the legendary general Lachit Barphukan, who decisively defeated the Mughal navy led by Ram Singh I.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31223","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following steel plant was established during second five year plan of India?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. Rourkela steel plant</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Bokaro steel plant</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Only I.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Rourkela Steel Plant, set up with German collaboration, was established during the Second Five-Year Plan (along with Bhilai and Durgapur). The Bokaro Steel Plant was established later, during the Third Five-Year Plan period (commissioned in 1972), so only Statement I is correct.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31224","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>On which food component does the salivary amylase act on in humans?</span></p>","option_1":"Proteins","option_2":"Fats","option_3":"Starch","option_4":"Vitamins","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Starch.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Salivary amylase (ptyalin), secreted by the salivary glands, begins the digestion of starch (a carbohydrate) in the mouth, breaking it down into maltose and dextrins.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31225","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Kaveripattinam was the main Chola port, is also known by which name?</span></p>","option_1":"Puhar","option_2":"Kandla","option_3":"Paradip","option_4":"Nhava Sheva","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Puhar.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Kaveripattinam, the principal port city of the ancient Chola kingdom located at the mouth of the Kaveri river, was also known as Puhar, and finds mention in Sangam literature such as the Silappatikaram.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31226","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What is the first step in the break-down of glucose in all organisms?</span></p>","option_1":"Conversion of glucose into carbon dioxide and water","option_2":"Conversion of pyruvate into ethanol","option_3":"Break-down of glucose into pyruvate","option_4":"Conversion of pyruvate into lactic acid","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Break-down of glucose into pyruvate.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Glucose breakdown begins with glycolysis, a universal process in all living organisms, in which one molecule of glucose (a 6-carbon compound) is broken down into two molecules of pyruvate (a 3-carbon compound). This is the first common step before aerobic or anaerobic respiration pathways diverge.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31227","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following is a traditional dance performed by the 'Wancho' tribe of Arunachal Pradesh?</span></p>","option_1":"Ozele","option_2":"Kummi","option_3":"Lambadi","option_4":"Dindi","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Ozele.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Ozele is a traditional dance performed by the Wancho tribe of Arunachal Pradesh, usually during festivals and important community occasions. Kummi and Lambadi are dance forms of South India, while Dindi is associated with Maharashtra/Andhra folk traditions.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31228","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which traditional dance form, known for its martial style and energetic movements, is performed in Odisha's Mayurbhanj district?</span></p>","option_1":"Chhau","option_2":"Dindi","option_3":"Julli","option_4":"Chari","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Chhau.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Chhau dance, particularly the Mayurbhanj style (performed without masks, unlike the Seraikella and Purulia styles), is a semi-classical Indian dance with martial, tribal, and folk traditions, known for its vigorous, energetic movements.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31229","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>_____ is a harvest festival of the Jharkhand.</span></p>","option_1":"Sohrai","option_2":"Shigmotsav","option_3":"Baishagu","option_4":"Halda","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Sohrai.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Sohrai is a major harvest festival celebrated by the tribal communities of Jharkhand, marked by cattle worship and elaborate wall paintings (Sohrai art) to celebrate the harvest season.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31230","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Kappad and Chal beaches are in which of the following state?</span></p>","option_1":"Kerala","option_2":"Gujarat","option_3":"Goa","option_4":"Andhra Pradesh","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Kerala.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Kappad beach (near Kozhikode, famous as the landing site of Vasco da Gama in 1498) and Chal beach are both located in Kerala.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31231","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In which Indian Union Territory is the 'Hemis Festival' celebrated?</span></p>","option_1":"Ladakh","option_2":"Chandigarh","option_3":"Puducherry","option_4":"Delhi","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Ladakh.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Hemis Festival is celebrated annually at the Hemis Monastery in Ladakh, commemorating the birth anniversary of Guru Padmasambhava, marked by masked Cham dances performed by monks.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31232","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which Mughal emperor ruled between 1754 and 1759?</span></p>","option_1":"Ahmad Shah","option_2":"Farrukh Siyar","option_3":"Shah Alam II","option_4":"Alamgir II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Alamgir II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Alamgir II ruled the Mughal Empire from 1754 to 1759, during a period when real power was held by his wazir Imad-ul-Mulk, and the empire was significantly weakened by internal strife and external invasions.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31233","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following statement is correct regarding Satellite?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. A satellite launched by man around any planet for our own purpose is called an artificial satellite.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. The moon is an example of artificial satellite.</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Only I.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Statement I is correct - an artificial satellite is one launched and placed in orbit by humans for a specific purpose (communication, navigation, etc.). Statement II is incorrect - the Moon is a natural satellite of Earth, not an artificial one.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31234","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>A constant force acts on an object of mass 6 kg for a duration of 5 seconds. It increases the object's velocity from 3 m/s to 8 m/s. Find the magnitude of the applied force.</span></p>","option_1":"6 N","option_2":"10 N","option_3":"15 N","option_4":"20 N","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 6 N.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Using F = ma, where acceleration a = (v-u)/t = (8-3)/5 = 1 m/s². Therefore, F = 6 kg × 1 m/s² = 6 N.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31235","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The legendary classical vocalist Kesarbai Kerkar was associated with which Gharana?</span></p>","option_1":"Patiala Gharana","option_2":"Kirana Gharana","option_3":"Banaras Gharana","option_4":"Jaipur-Atrauli Gharana","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Jaipur-Atrauli Gharana.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Kesarbai Kerkar, one of the foremost Hindustani classical vocalists of the 20th century, was a disciple of Ustad Alladiya Khan and belonged to the Jaipur-Atrauli Gharana, known for its emphasis on rare ragas and rhythmic complexity.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31236","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following is the indirect source to provides information about the interior of the Earth?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. Volcano</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Earthquake</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Only II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Earthquake waves (seismic waves) are the primary indirect source of information about the Earth's interior, as their behavior while passing through different layers reveals the density and state of matter within. Volcanic eruptions are considered a direct source since they bring molten material from within the Earth to the surface.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31237","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What term describes a measure of the response of the ear to sound?</span></p>","option_1":"Frequency","option_2":"Loudness","option_3":"Pitch","option_4":"Intensity","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Loudness.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Loudness is the subjective perception of sound intensity by the human ear - it describes how the ear responds to and interprets the intensity of a sound wave, and is measured in decibels (dB).</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31238","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following is NOT an example of Indirect Tax?</span></p>","option_1":"Corporation tax","option_2":"Entertainment tax","option_3":"Service tax","option_4":"Sales tax","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Corporation tax.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Corporation tax (tax on company profits) is a direct tax, as its burden cannot be shifted to another party. Entertainment tax, service tax, and sales tax are all indirect taxes, as their burden can be passed on to the consumer.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31239","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>About 80% of the total road length in India are categorised as _____.</span></p>","option_1":"National Highways","option_2":"State Highways","option_3":"District Roads","option_4":"Rural Roads","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Rural Roads.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Rural roads constitute the largest share of India's total road network, accounting for roughly 80% of the total road length, connecting villages to towns and cities, largely developed under schemes like the Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31240","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As per Department of Atomic Energy, India has constituted _____ of the global total thorium reserves.</span></p>","option_1":"31%","option_2":"21%","option_3":"44%","option_4":"54%","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 21%.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As per the Department of Atomic Energy, India possesses about 21% of the world's total thorium reserves, primarily in the form of monazite sands along its coastal regions, making it a key resource for India's future nuclear energy programme.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31241","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following books has been written by V.S. Naipaul?</span></p>","option_1":"Eunuch Park","option_2":"Half a Life","option_3":"The Princess","option_4":"A Passage to England","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Half a Life.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Half a Life' (2001) is a novel by Nobel laureate V.S. Naipaul, tracing the life of its protagonist Willie Chandran across India, England, and Africa.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31242","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Who is the author of the book 'Brahma's Hair'?</span></p>","option_1":"Karan Singh","option_2":"Manmohan Singh","option_3":"Atal Bihari Vajpayee","option_4":"Maneka Gandhi","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Maneka Gandhi.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Brahma's Hair: On the Mythology of Indian Plants' is a book authored by Maneka Gandhi, exploring the mythological and cultural significance of various Indian plants.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31243","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which among the following temples is located in Mount Abu?</span></p>","option_1":"Kashi Vishvanath Temple","option_2":"Golden Temple","option_3":"Dilwara Jain Temple","option_4":"Thousand Pillar Temple","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Dilwara Jain Temple.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Dilwara Jain Temples, renowned for their exquisite marble carvings, are located at Mount Abu, Rajasthan, and were built between the 11th and 13th centuries CE.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31244","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following is the largest dome of India?</span></p>","option_1":"Adhai Din Ka Jhopra","option_2":"Gol Gumbaz","option_3":"Data Darbar","option_4":"Taj Mahal","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Gol Gumbaz.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Gol Gumbaz in Bijapur, Karnataka, the mausoleum of Mohammed Adil Shah, has the largest dome in India (and the second largest pre-modern dome in the world), with a diameter of about 44 metres, built without any pillar support.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31245","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"838","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In India, which of the following colour is used to denote the earth wire in the supply?</span></p>","option_1":"White","option_2":"Red","option_3":"Green","option_4":"Black","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Green.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As per Indian electrical wiring standards, green (or green-yellow) colour insulation is used to denote the earth (ground) wire, which provides a safety path for fault currents.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""}]